  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nA prolonged period of rain and some storms will arrive to start\nnext week. The western U.S. trough will eventually eject into \nthe plains, enhancing the low and midlevel jet in the warm \nsector. At this range, deterministic runs bring MUCAPE values \naround the 300 J/kg range into southern Iowa. The potential for \nsurface- based severe weather is heavily challenged by morning \nrain and lingering cloud cover. Moderate wrap-around rain stays \nin the state for Tuesday and partially into Wednesday. NBM QMD \nguidance shows that much of the state has around a 50% chance of\nexceeding 2\" of rain, which matches the early convective nature\nas well as the very high PWATs (1.5\") by November standards. \nThe progressive nature of convection and the long-duration of \nrain will reduce potential hydrologic impacts. Highs will cool \ninto the 50s following the system's departure and the upstream \npattern will remain active and amplified.",
